WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.306
Updating a runtime or changing a model can affect behavior, performance, compatibility,

00:00:04.306 --> 00:00:05.358
and support expectations.

00:00:05.358 --> 00:00:08.162
A new runtime version may change service behavior.

00:00:08.162 --> 00:00:13.069
A new model tag may change response style, memory needs, latency, or application assumptions.

00:00:13.069 --> 00:00:16.224
A configuration change may alter storage paths, network exposure,

00:00:16.224 --> 00:00:18.678
or how a model is kept loaded.

00:00:18.678 --> 00:00:22.534
Teams should document known-good combinations of runtime version, model version, hardware,

00:00:22.534 --> 00:00:23.936
configuration, and integration behavior.

00:00:23.936 --> 00:00:27.091
This does not need to be a heavy process.

00:00:27.091 --> 00:00:31.297
It can be a short operational note that records what was tested,

00:00:31.297 --> 00:00:34.452
who approved shared use, what workflows depend on it,

00:00:34.452 --> 00:00:37.957
and what rollback option exists if users see a problem.

00:00:37.957 --> 00:00:41.462
Backups should focus on the assets that matter for recovery.

00:00:41.462 --> 00:00:43.565
Model files can often be re-pulled,

00:00:43.565 --> 00:00:46.369
but undocumented configuration decisions are harder to recover.

00:00:46.369 --> 00:00:49.524
Modelfiles, service configuration notes, approved model inventories, integration settings,

00:00:49.524 --> 00:00:50.225
routing assumptions,

00:00:50.225 --> 00:00:55.133
and support documentation may be more important than a copy of every model file.

00:00:55.133 --> 00:00:58.638
Rollback planning matters when shared tools depend on the environment.

00:00:58.638 --> 00:01:02.844
If a runtime update causes errors or a model change alters behavior,

00:01:02.844 --> 00:01:08.453
operators should know how to restore the previous known-good state or route users to an alternative.

00:01:08.453 --> 00:01:11.257
Change control is not about slowing down experimentation.

00:01:11.257 --> 00:01:15.814
It is about making operational changes clear enough that teams know what changed,

00:01:15.814 --> 00:01:20.020
why it changed, and how to respond if it does not work.
